    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the ratio of the volume of a cube with an edge length of 6 inches to the volume of a cube with an edge length of 1 foot, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the volume of the first cube (radius 6 inches).\n2. Calculate the volume of the second cube (radius 1 foot).\n3. Determine the ratio of the two volumes by dividing the volume of the second cube by the volume of the first cube.\n\nFirst, we start with the volume of the first cube. The formula for the volume of a cube with edge length \\( s \\) is given by \\( s^3 \\). Let's denote the edge length of the first cube as \\( s_1 = 6 \\) inches. Therefore, the volume of the first cube is:\n\n\\[\nv_1 = s_1^3 = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the volume of the second cube. The edge length of the second cube is 1 foot. There are 12 inches in a foot, so the edge length of the second cube in inches is \\( s_2 = 1 \\times 12 = 12 \\) inches. Therefore, the volume of the second cube is:\n\n\\[\nv_2 = s_2^3 = 12^3 = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the ratio of the volume of the second cube to the volume of the first cube:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{v_2}{v_1} = \\frac{1728}{216}\n\\]\n\nWe simplify this fraction by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1728 and 216. Both numbers are divisible by 1728, so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1728}{216} = \\frac{1728 \\div 1728}{216 \\div 1728} = \\frac{1}{1.25} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{5}{4}} = \\frac{4}{5}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the volume of the cube with an edge length of 6 inches to the volume of the cube with an edge length of 1 foot is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{4}{5}}\n\\]",
